 Bahia, Paraná, Athletico-PR, Vitória, Sport, Chapecoense, Ceará. Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo/Vasco were NOT in FIFA 19 (licensing). That's a data reality — \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" may return \"no players found at Flamengo in FIFA dataset; note licensing exclusion\". Interesting quirk worth handling gracefully.\n\nLet me check the full list of Brazilian clubs in FIFA data and also count players by nationality. Also check \"Atletico Mineiro\" spelling in FIFA: \"Atlético Mineiro\". \n\n**Structural plan:**\n\nFiles:\n- `soccer/__init__.py` — package exports\n- `soccer/normalize.py` — fold, date parsing, team name cleaning, alias table, TeamRegistry (resolve query → keys)\n- `soccer/models.py` — dataclasses Match, Player, TeamRecord\n- `soccer/loader.py` — load all 6 CSVs, build unified Match list with dedup, Player list, TeamRegistry\n- `soccer/service.py` — query functions: search_matches, head_to_head, team_stats, standings, biggest_wins, competition_stats, search_players, players_by_club, team_profile, derby_matches, list_competitions\n- `server.py` — FastMCP server wrapping service functions as tools\n- `tests/conftest.py` — fixtures (loaded dataset)\n- `tests/test_*.py` — BDD GWT tests\n\nThe task instruction says \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" Hmm. To be safe, maybe keep everything flat in the current directory? \"current directory\" as opposed to elsewhere on the machine — I think subpackages are fine and more professional. But there's risk a grader literally wants files in cwd. The safest interpretation: all files under the repo (cwd) — subdirectories are within the current directory tree. I'll use a package `soccer/` + `tests/`. Actually, let me reconsider — \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — I'll interpret as the repo. Subdirs are standard for Python projects; \"builds and tests pass\" is what matters.\n\n**Dedup design:**\nCompetition canonical IDs:\n- 'brasileirao' — Serie A. Sources: novo (2003-2019), br_matches (2012-2022), br_foot Serie A (2014-2023). Dedup key: (season, home_key, away_key) — unique per fixture in double round-robin. Preference order: br_matches (has round+datetime+state) > novo (round+stadium+date) > br_foot (stats+date+time).\n  Wait, one fixture (home,away) per season — yes in double round-robin each ordered pair meets exactly once. \n- 'copa_do_brasil' — Sources: cup file (2012-2021), br_foot Copa do Brasil (2014-2023). Dedup: cups have two legs; same home team could host twice in a season (rare). Key: (season, home_key, away_key, date)? dates could differ slightly between sources for same match? Let me check overlap agreement on cup. Also round strings in cup file: \"1\", \"2\", ..., \"final\"? Let me check round values in cup file and stage values in Libertadores.\n- 'libertadores' — single source.\n- 'serie_b', 'serie_c' — from br_foot only. Wait — br file (Brasileirao_Matches) is Serie A only? Yes 380 matches/season. novo also Serie A only. So serie_b/serie_c only from BR-Foot. But BR-Foot 'Serie A' overlaps the brasileirao sources.\n\nHmm, wait: should 'serie_a' (BR-Foot tournament) be merged into 'brasileirao'? The spec lists competitions as \"Brasileirão, Copa do Brasil, Libertadores\" but data coverage includes Serie B/C. I'll expose competitions:\n- \"Brasileirão Serie A\" (id 'brasileirao') — merged from 3 sources with dedup: seasons 2003-2023!\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" (id 'copa_do_brasil') — merged from 2 sources: 2012-2023\n- \"Copa Libertadores\" (id 'libertadores') — 2013-2022\n- \"Série B\" (id 'serie_b') — 2014-2023\n- \"Série C\" (id 'serie_c') — 2014-2023\n\nLet me now check cup file round values + overlap between cup sources, and Libertadores stage values. Also check whether br_foot Copa rows agree with cup file (numeric). And FIFA data: Brazilian clubs full list, plus check \"Overall\" type, positions. Also check what 'NA'/'-' goals rows look like (unplayed matches) — flag them as unplayed.\n\nAlso: 