e suffixes; in BR-Foot they don't (\"Boavista\"). So keys differ (\"boavistasportclubrj\" vs \"boavista\")! That's why only 8 matched. My bad — the earlier collision analysis properly stripped suffixes. So for cross-source matching, keys must strip state suffixes and handle collisions via canonical mapping. OK — the TeamRegistry is definitely needed: canonical key includes state (from any source that provides it); sources without state (BR-Foot) resolve via base name → if base maps to exactly one suffixed key, use it; if ambiguous, use match-count majority or keep separate... e.g., BR-Foot \"Botafogo\" — Botafogo-RJ or Botafogo-PB? BR-Foot Serie A/B has Botafogo-RJ (Serie A) and Botafogo-PB (Serie C?) — both appear as \"Botafogo\"! Ugh. In BR-Foot, disambiguation must be contextual (by tournament: Serie A → Botafogo-RJ; Serie C → Botafogo-PB?) — but Botafogo-PB played Serie B/C. Hmm.\n\nPragmatic approach for BR-Foot ambiguity: when a stateless name maps to multiple canonical keys, pick the one that appears in the SAME tournament+season context. E.g., \"Botafogo\" in Serie A 2015 → Botafogo-RJ (PB never in Serie A in that era). \"Botafogo\" in Serie C 2016 → Botafogo-PB (RJ wasn't in Serie C). This works if, within a season+tournament, only one variant participated. Check: Botafogo-RJ in Serie B? Botafogo-RJ was in Serie B in 2021 (relegated 2020)! And Botafogo-PB in Serie B 2016? Possibly. So within Serie B 2021, \"Botafogo\" = RJ. Within Serie B 2016, is Botafogo-PB there? Botafogo-PB played Serie B 2016 (yes, they were in Serie B around 2016-2019!). Was Botafogo-RJ in Serie B 2016? No (RJ was in Serie A 2016). So per (tournament, season) disambiguation works.\n\nSimpler robust rule: within each (tournament, season) group, if a stateless name maps to N canonical candidates, check which candidate appears in that same competition-season from OTHER sources; if exactly one → pick it. Otherwise keep as separate \"unknown-state\" entity with base name. Since queries mainly target major teams and Serie A, the edge cases (Botafogo in Serie C etc.) rarely matter. But standings for Serie B/C would be affected by Botafogo splitting into two entities within one season — only if BOTH Botafogos played the same Serie B season. Let me check quickly whether any (tournament, season) has genuinely ambiguous stateless names. Actually — simpler: for BR-Foot (the only stateless source), I can disambiguate per (tournament, season): candidates = canonical keys from OTHER sources (cup file has state, br file has state, novo has state) observed in ANY season of that tournament, or by overall match counts. Let me check the ambiguous set: bases with multiple states: atletico, america, botafogo, bragantino, comercial, flamengo, guarani, internacional, juventude, nautico, operario, riobranco, santacruz, santos, saoraimundo, ypiranga, vitoria (from collision list), + others.\n\n\"Internacional\" — Internacional-RS vs Internacional-SC (cup). \"Santos\" — Santos-SP vs Santos-AP! \"Vitoria\" — Vitoria-BA vs... \"Vitoria da Conquista\"? no that's a different base. OK.\n\nFor BR-Foot \"Santos\" in Serie A → Santos-SP obviously. Internacional in Serie A → RS. Fine.\n\nGiven time constraints, here's my disambiguation algorithm for stateless source names (BR-Foot):\n- base = fold(strip-state(name))\n- candidates = all canonical keys (base+state) known from suffixed sources ∪ {stateless key itself}\n- If exactly one → resolved.\n- If multiple → pick by tournament context: candidates seen in same tournament (any season) from suffixed sources... e.g. Botafogo-PB seen in Copa do Brasil (cup file); Botafogo-RJ seen in brasileirao + copa. For Serie B context — both could be \"seen in copa\"... Hmm not enough. Better: rank candidates by total match count in suffixed sources and pick the majority? That would map ALL BR-Foot \"Botafogo\" → Botafogo-RJ, wrongly crediting PB's Serie C matches to RJ. But honestly, for a demo MCP server, Botafogo-PB Serie C stats being conflated with Botafogo-RJ is a data quality wart. The spec's success criteria focus on major-team queries.\n\nActually — better idea: use *tournament + season coexistence*: for each (tournament, season), resolve stateless names by checking which candidates appear in that exact (tournament, season) from SUFFIXED sources. Since cup file covers copa 2012-2021 and br/novo cover serie A, this resolves copa + serie A contexts. For Serie B/C (BR-Foot exclusive), no suffixed source → fall back to: candidates that appear in the same tournament in OTHER seasons? Botafogo-PB in Serie B 2016-2019 (BR-Foot itself stateless...). \n\nOK here's the thing — within BR-Foot itself, if BOTH Botafogos played the same Serie B season, the data would show \"Botafogo\" playing 40+ matches (both teams' fixtures merged) — detectable but hard to split. Let me just check quickly if e.g. Serie B 2016 \"Botafogo\" match count > 38 for one... Actually Botafogo-PB in Serie B: matches vs 19 opponents home+away = 38 matches. If both RJ and PB were in Serie B same year, \"Botafogo\" would appear ~76 times. Let me check the ambiguous bases in BR-Foot per tournament-season match counts. If ≤ 38-ish (or just \"reasonable\"), I'll resolve by majority-candidate heuristic and move on. Actually simpler: resolve stateless BR-Foot names per (tournament, season): among candidates, pick the one whose *participation* makes sense... \n\nYou know what — I'm overengineering. Let me check the actual data: for ambiguous bases, in each (tournament, season), how many matches does the stateless name have? If always ≤38 for serie B and one candidate clearly dominates historically, use per-(tournament,season) pick via: candidate must be a team that played that tournament in a nearby season in a suffixed source; if still ambiguous → majority by total count. Let me just look at the numbers.\n\n4. **Cup \"finals\" mapping**: round number → name per season. Let me compute per-season max round & final identity from data: In each sea