n, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\nasync with stdio_client(params) as (read, write):\n    async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n        await session.initialize()\n        tools = await session.list_tools()\n        res = await session.call_tool('search_matches', {'team':'Flamengo'})\n```\nAnyio/asyncio in pytest — pytest doesn't run async natively; use `asyncio.run()` inside a sync test. Fine.\n\nNow — write it all. Order: normalize.py, models.py, derbies.py, loader.py, service.py, server.py, then tests, then README, then run.\n\nOne more design point — **service function signatures** (shared by MCP tools and tests):\n\n```python\nclass SoccerService:\n    def __init__(self, data: SoccerData)\n    def search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, stage=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=15) -> str\n    def head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None) -> str\n    def team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all') -> str\n    def team_profile(team) -> str\n    def league_standings(competition='brasileirao', season=None, venue='all', limit=None) -> str\n    def finals(competition) -> str\n    def biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10) -> str\n    def competition_info(competition=None) -> str  (None → overview of all)\n    def search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, min_potential=None, sort='overall', limit=15) -> str\n    def players_by_club(nationality='Brazil', brazilian_clubs_only=True, limit=15) -> str\n    def derby_matches(derby=None, season=None) -> str\n    def list_tools_help() -> str  (maybe: overview)\n```\n\nOutput formats follow spec examples. Let me also expose `resolve_team` for tests.\n\n**Errors**: unknown team → \"Team 'X' not found. Similar: ...\" (difflib). Ambiguous → list candidates. Unknown competition → list competitions.\n\n**Match counting for \"head-to-head in dataset\"**: only played matches count for W/D/L.\n\n**Standings relegated**: bottom 4 (mark them). Only for brasileirao where season complete-ish (38 rounds). For serie_b relegated = bottom 4 as well; serie_c different format — just mark bottom 2? I'll mark bottom 4 for A/B, bottom 2 for C with label \"relegation zone\" generality... Actually keep: champion marked; bottom-4 marked \"Relegated\" for brasileirao; for serie_b also bottom 4; serie_c — no relegation within dataset (Serie D not covered) → just no relegation note. Hmm, simpler: relegation note only for brasileirao & serie_b.\n\nAlso **access field** (\"Which team has the best away record?\" — league_standings(venue='away')). ✓\n\n**\"Compare the 2018 and 2019 seasons\"** — competition_info per season gives avg goals etc. I'll include per-season avg goals in competition_info(season=...)? Add optional season param to competition_info. OK.\n\n**\"Show me all derbies in 2023\"** — derby_matches(season=2023) — derbies in brasileirao etc. But 2023 serie A comes from BR-Foot merge ✓.\n\n**Half-time results**: BR-Foot ht_result/at_result (WON/LOST/DRAW per team) — hmm, \"ht_diff\"/\"at_diff\" are diffs. I'll store stats: corners, shots, attacks, ht_diff? Keep stats = {home_corners, away_corners, home_shots, away_shots, home_attacks, away_attacks, total_corners}. Display in match lines when available? Match line already long; include stats only in search_matches when `include_stats=True`? Skip — keep stats accessible via search_matches output line suffix \"(stats: corners 6-4, shots 13-8)\" when available? Nice touch — include compactly when present.\n\nWait, one thing on ht_result semantics: row 2: home=Flamengo 0-1 Sao Paulo; ht_diff=-1, ht_result=LOST, at_result=WON. So ht = home team, at = away team ✓ consistent.\n\n**models.py**:\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass TeamRef: key: str; display: str\n@dataclass\nclass MatchStats: home_corners/away_corners/home_shots/away_shots/home_attacks/away_attacks/total_corners (int|None)\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    competition, competition_display, season, date (date|None), time (str|None),\n    stage (str|None)  # 'final','semifinals',... or None\n    round_label (str|None)  # 'Round 22' etc\n    home: TeamRef, away: TeamRef, home_goals/away_goals: int|None,\n    venue: str|None, stats: MatchStats|None, source: str,\n    played: bool (goals not None)\n    def describe(self) -> str  # \"2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Round 22)\"\n@dataclass\nclass Player: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, preferred_foot, value, wage...\n```\n\nPlayer fields: ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Preferred Foot, Value, Wage, Height, Weight. Keep those.\n\n**Date parsing**: \n```python\ndef parse_date(s): formats: '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S', '%Y-%m-%d', '%d/%m/%Y', 'NA'/'' → None\n```\n\n**TeamRegistry internals**:\n```python\nclass TeamRegistry:\n    teams: dict[key → Team(display, base, state, country, key)]\n    alias: dict[folded_alias → key]   # base folds, full-name folds\n    base_counts: Counter[key] (participations from suffixed sources)\n    def register(raw, state_hint=None, count_source=True) → key\n    def resolve(query) → Resolution(single=key | ambiguous=[...] | notfound, suggestions)\n```\n\nRegister algorithm for raw name:\n1. parse_name(raw) → (base, state|None, country|None, pretty_base)\n2. key = f\"{base}-{state.lower()}\" if state else (f\"{base}-{country.lower()}\" if country else alias.get(base) or base)\n   Wait — careful: for stateless, resolution needs context. register() called during load; for stateless names: check alias table first (atleticomineiro→atletico-mg etc.); then if base maps to exactly ONE known suffixed key → that key; else (ambiguous or unknown) → coexistence/majority (for loader rows w/ context) or new stateless entity key=base.\n3. Track display variants.\n\nOrder of registration matters: register all SUFFIXED names first (br, novo w/ UF, cup, BR-Foot suffixed, lib) → then stateless with context. Loader phases:\n- Phase A: br file (suffixed), novo (UF columns), cup (suffixed), lib (mostly suffixed or plain-foreign), BR-Foot suffixed names (\"Botafogo RJ\").\n  Hmm — lib foreign teams: \"Boca Juniors\" plain, \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Toluca\", \"Tigres\", \"River Plate\", \"San Lorenzo\", \"Lanús\", \"Olimpia-PAR\"... plain names register as stateless unknown → new entity base key ✓ (no collision with Brazilian bases).\n- Phase B: BR-Foot stateless league rows (with coexistence context: season + league-family) & copa rows (majority/alias), FIFA clubs (alias/majority).\n\nCoexistence context: league_family = {'serie_a','serie_b','serie_c'} mutually exclusive per (season). From Phase A I know participation: (league, season) → set of keys. br file gives serie_a 2012-2022; novo serie_a 2003-2019. For a stateless BR-Foot row (base B, league L, season S) with candidates C = known keys with base B: filter candidates NOT participating in another league in season S (league family from phase A — but phase A only covers serie_a!). For Serie B/C rows, phase A has no participation info → coexistence can't help within B/C. BUT cross-league exclusion still works partially: e.g., ambiguous \"bragantino\" in Serie B 2014: candidates bragantino-sp, bragantino-pa. Phase A: bragantino-sp in serie_a?? no (they were Serie B). bragantino-pa? never serie_a. So no exclusion possible → majority → sp (assuming sp has more total appearances). ✓ \nAnd \"internacional\" Serie B 2017: candidates rs, sc. Phase A: internacional-RS in serie_a? 2017 — Internacional was RELEGATED after 2016 → Serie B 2017 ✓ so RS not in serie_a 2017; SC never. No exclusion → majority → RS ✓ (RS majority).\n\"flamengo\" Serie A 2014 (BR-Foot): candidates rj, pi. Phase A: flamengo-rj in serie_a 2014 ✓ (from br file) → resolves to RJ even without majority ✓.\n\nOK the coexistence rule only shines for Serie A, majority handles the rest. \n\n**Majority for loader (Phase B)**: resolve to the candidate with most Phase-A registrations (participations); tie/unknown → new stateless entity? No — pick max; if no candidates at all → new entity (stateless key = base).\n\nHmm — one more subtlety: BR-Foot copa rows 2022-2023 add NEW teams not in phase A (small clubs from state BN?? states like \"Santa Cruz - RN\"...). Copa file (phase A) has lots of small clubs suffixed ✓. BR-Foot copa 2022-2023 small clubs stateless (\"Americano\"?), no candidates → new stateless entities. Fine.\n\nAnd user-facing resolve(): alias → exact key → unique base → dominance(≥75%?) → substring unique → disambiguation list / difflib suggestions.\n\nWait — should user-facing \"atletico\" (no dominance) auto-pick? The spec examples never ask bare \"atletico\". Disambiguation is better UX for LLM. But careful — dominance threshold: botafogo rj vs pb: rj ≈ 415+pb≈50 → 89% → auto RJ. santos sp vs ap: ~95% sp. flamengo rj vs pi: ~98%. internacional rs vs sc: rs ~600, sc ~8 → 98%. guarani sp vs ce: sp more? Guarani-SP (Campinas, serie A participant) vs Guarani-CE (tiny) → sp dominates ✓. america mg vs rn: mg ~400 rn ~40 → 91% → auto MG. Hmm — América-RN vs América-MG both real clubs; 91% dominance auto-picks MG. Acceptable (\"América\" bare usually means MG in serie A context). atletico mg/pr/go: mg ~35%, pr ~30%, go ~20% → disambiguate ✓. bragantino sp vs pa: sp dominates ✓ (good — RB Bragantino). santacruz pe vs rn vs rs: pe dominates ✓. vitoria... unique (vitoria-ba only? \"Vitória da Conquista\" different base) ✓. nautico pe vs rr: pe dominates ✓. operario: ms/mt/pr hmm — operário-MS (VG most played) vs PR vs MT — no dominance → disambiguate. comercial ms vs pi → ms dominates? both small; counts low; maybe no dominance → disambiguate. Fine.\n\nThreshold 0.75 with the top candidate — else disambiguation list. \n\n**Now, matches storage**: list of Match + indexes for speed (not strictly needed; 25k matches linear scan is fine ~ms). Actually total matches after merge: brasileirao ~7666 (2003-2023: novo 6886 − overlap 1924 + br 4180... let me not precompute; whatever) + copa ~1900 + lib 1255 + serie_b 3677 + serie_c 1807 ≈ 16k. Linear scans fine.\n\n**Sorting matches**: by date desc default in search (most recent first) — spec example lists 2023 first then older ✓.\n\n**Formatting helpers**: \n```python\ndef fmt_match(m): \n  score = f\"{m.home_goals}-{m.away_goals}\" if played else \"vs (not recorded)\"\n  date part: m.date or 'date N/A'\n  comp part: competition_display + (round_label or stage)\n  stats suffix if present\n```\n\n**Standings computation**: from played matches of a league-season: per team: P W D L GF GA GD Pts. Sort: Pts, W, GD, GF, name. Also venue filter: home-only table (team as home), away-only. Relegated: positions -4: for brasileirao/serie_b if ≥ 12 teams... eh mark bottom 4 when ≥16 teams.\n\nEdge: 2020 serie A — 2020 br file rows all played? 2020 had 380 rows... earlier NA only 2022(81)+2016(1) → 2020 fine.\n\n**Competition info**: matches count, seasons list (min-max), teams count, avg goals, home/draw/away win rates, goals total. Per-season when season given.\n\n**biggest_wins**: sort by margin desc, then goals desc. Across competitions or filtered.\n\n**finals(competition)**:\n- libertadores: stage == 'final' matches grouped by season → each season: teams, agg score, winner (two-leg agg with... no away-goals info? Libertadores used away goals until 2021! Computing two-leg winner: aggregate; if tie → away goals (pre-2021?) — 2018 final: Boca 2-2 River; River 3-1 Boca → River agg 5-3 ✓ no tie. 2013: Olimpia 2-0 Atl-MG; Atl-MG 2-0 Olimpia → agg 2-2! Away goals: Atl-MG scored 2 away, Olimpia 0 away → Atlético-MG... but REAL winner 2013 = Atlético-MG (won on away goals ✓!). So implement: aggregate → if tie, away goals (for finals before 2021; post-2021 ties went to extra time/penalties which dataset doesn't record — e.g. no such tie occurs in data? 2016: IDV 1-1 AtlNacional; AtlNacional 1-0 IDV → agg 2-1 ✓. 2019/2020 single-leg. All decidable except agg-ties pre-2021 → away goals → all resolvable ✓. 2021: no final data → report semifinalists + note. 2022: NA final → teams + \"result not in dataset\".)\n- copa_do_brasil: final round per season = max played round from cup-file rows; matches of that round → two-leg agg; winner (copa never used away goals — level agg → penalties; 2017 final: Flamengo 1-1 Cruzeiro; Cruzeiro 0-0 Flamengo → agg 1-1 → penalties (Cruzeiro won 3-0... actually 2017 Cruzeiro won on penalties ✓). So agg tie → \"won on penalties (winner not determinable from scores alone)\" → but known: 2017 Cruzeiro. Hmm — data can't tell. I'll output \"aggregate level — decided on penalties\" without naming winner for those. Honest. 2021+: cup file NA; merged BR-Foot rows have no rounds... The finals tool for copa 2021-2023: find final via BR-Foot: last distinct pairing(s) by date at season end? I said I'd infer finals from BR-Foot tail: 2021: Atlético-MG vs Athletico (2 matches, agg 6-1) ✓; 2022: Corinthians vs Flamengo (2 matches agg 2-1 Flamengo ✓); 2023: São Paulo vs Flamengo (agg 2-1 SP ✓). Inference rule: group copa matches per season by (pair-set) — the two teams whose matches have the LATEST dates at season tail and are 1-2 matches... Risky to generalize; but for 2021-2023 I can implement: for seasons where cup file has no final-round data, take the last TWO teams by latest match dates: specifically, matches on/after the last-but-7-days? Ugh.\n\nSimpler heuristic that works for 2021-2023: per season, consider matches sorted by date; the final = the pairing(s) among the last 2 match-dates involving exactly 2 distinct teams, where those teams won their semifinals... Too clever. Practical approach: the final tie = the distinct team-pair appearing in the last N matches (N=2) of the season (two-legged 2021-2022, two-legged 2023 ✓ all two-leg). Check: 2023 last two matches by date: 2023-09-17 Flamengo 0-1 São Paulo; 2023-09-24 São Paulo 1-1 Flamengo → pair {SP, Flamengo} ✓. 2022: 10-13 & 10-20 Corinthians/Flamengo ✓. 2021: 12-12 & 12-16 AtlMG/Athletico ✓. And for 2014-2020 where cup file already provides finals via rounds, use the cup-file finals (authoritative). But what if BR-Foot's last-2 heuristic picks wrong for 2014-2020? I only apply it when cup file lacks played finals that season. ✓ Also 2012/2013: cup file has finals ✓.\n\nBut wait — what about the 2021 NA round-4 rows polluting? The finals tool ignores unplayed rows; 2021 cup-file rows are all NA → no played final round → fall back to BR-Foot tail heuristic ✓.\n\nAnd search_matches with stage='final' → same finals logic (or map stage filter). I'll keep `finals(competition)` tool separate, and search_matches stage param matches round_label/stage substring (\"final\" matches stage 'final' and round_label 'Final').\n\n**Libertadores 2022 fixup**: row (Flamengo vs Athletico, final, NA date) → season 2022, date None. In finals output: \"2022 Final: Flamengo vs Athletico-PR — result not recorded in dataset\". Should I name the winner Flamengo (well-known)? I'll add a NOTES dict with curated facts for exactly these gaps, clearly marked as \"outside dataset\" notes: {('libertadores', 2022): \"Flamengo won the 2022 final 1-0 aet (not recorded in dataset)\"}. Hmm — I'd rather stay data-pure... But \"Who won the 2022 Libertadores?\" is exactly the kind of question the MCP should answer. The spec says \"Integration: MCP server connected to an LLM for query processing\" — the LLM can know this. I'll include the curated note clearly labeled. Yes — knowledge-graph servers commonly have such notes. Keep it minimal (only this one fact + FIFA licensing note).\n\nAlso similar: 2021 lib final missing (Palmeiras beat Flamengo 2-1 aet). Add note too. And 2021 copa final: BR-Foot has it ✓ no note needed.\n\nOK — and \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings 2019: Flamengo 90 pts ✓ (data).\n\n**Tests expected values** (I'll verify while writing tests by computing):\n- 2019 standings: Flamengo champion. Let me verify points from data during test run (the spec example says 90 pts — trust data; assert champion == Flamengo and pts == 90).\n- Gre-Nal matches exist.\n- Neymar Jr: overall 92, nationality Brazil, club PSG ✓ (row shown).\n- h2h Flamengo-Fluminense counts >0.\n- biggest win: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (spec example mentions \"2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (Libertadores)\" — hmm that example mixes; whatever the data says).\n\nAlright — also **novo 'BH' state** quirk: register with UF 'BH'?? base+state \"guarani-bh\"?? wait which team is BH? novo states include BH — probably a data typo. If a team registers with weird state BH, it becomes a separate entity. Let me check which novo team has BH... I'll handle: state must be in STATES set else ignore state (treat as stateless). BH not a Brazilian state ✓ → ignored → resolved via majority. \n\nAlso careful — novo UF vs name suffix conflict: \"Athletico-PR\" name suffix PR + Mandante_UF PR consistent ✓.\n\n**BOM**: all files opened with encoding='utf-8-sig' ✓. The fifa first unnamed column '' — DictReader key '' — ignore (it's a row index).\n\n**Jersey Number**: might be empty for some → None.\n\nLet me now write all the code. I'll write files one by one. Start with normalize.py.\n\nActually one more decision: package name. `brazilian_soccer` (no accents in module name) ✓. Server file `server.py` at root. Tests in `tests/`. pytest.ini: testpaths=tests. requirements.txt: mcp>=2.0.\n\nLet me write normalize.py now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b81a8bc001dCpsq0bHc23FId","sessionID":"ses_fa4885bb0ffe9T9m197C2RPPvh","messageID":"msg_05b7f5cd9001S8OijFsNnpTuFD"}}
