)] = set of keys`. For a BR-Foot row (base B, league L∈{serie_a→'brasileirao', serie_b, serie_c}, season S): candidates minus keys playing a DIFFERENT league family member in season S. Since suffixed sources only cover brasileirao, participation only exists there. E.g., flamengo Serie A 2014: candidates {flamengorj, flamengopi}; participation[2014][brasileirao] contains flamengorj (from br file) — exclude candidates in ANOTHER league (not applicable — brasileirao IS the current league) hmm wait the exclusion logic: candidates that play a different league in season S can't be this team. flamengorj plays brasileirao 2014 = SAME league → keep. flamengopi plays nothing known → keep?? Then still 2 candidates → dominance → RJ ✓. Hmm — coexistence adds value only when: ambiguous team's candidate A plays league X in season S while row is league Y≠X → exclude A. E.g., bragantino Serie B 2014: candidates {bragantinosp, bragantinopa}. Neither in serie_a 2014 (bragantinosp was in serie B 2014! not in br file which is serie A only) → no exclusion → dominance → sp ✓ anyway. So coexistence rarely fires; dominance does the work. SIMPLIFY: rely on dominance only; skip coexistence. \n\nWait — one case where dominance fails: Internacional Serie B 2017 → dominance RS ✓ (RS has way more appearances overall). Juventude Serie B 2023 → RS ✓. Ypiranga Serie C → RS vs AP: counts? Both small. Let me check: cup \"Ypiranga - RS\" vs \"Ypiranga - AP\" counts. If AP > RS, dominance picks AP wrongly (Serie C 2016-17 ypiranga = RS club Erechim... actually WAIT — was it? Ypiranga-AP (Macapá) played Serie C 2016?? Hmm. Ypiranga-RS played Serie C 2015-2016-2017? and Ypiranga-AP played Serie C 2016?? I don't remember. Let me check the actual counts and just accept dominance result — it's a 16-match detail in Serie C 2016-17. Fine — dominance-only, document limitation in README.)\n\nHmm, hold on — one more important case: BR-Foot \"Botafogo\" appears spelled \"Botafogo RJ\"/\"Botafogo PB\"/\"Botafogo SP