ase \"bragantino\". Cup \"Bragantino - PA\" → \"bragantinopa\" ✓ added under base \"bragantino\". So candidates = {redbullbragantinosp, bragantinopa} → len 2 → ranked by appearances: redbullbragantinosp appearances: br file \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" (2020-2022: ~114) + cup \"Red Bull Bragantino - SP\" + cup \"Bragantino - SP\" (suffix-aliased, counted ✓)... bragantinopa: cup ~10. Dominance = redbull/(redbull+pa) — should be ≥ 0.75 → resolve ✓!! But it DIDN'T — the matches created a separate... wait, the created matches show home display \"Bragantino\" — under WHICH key? Let me check: is there a bare \"bragantino\" entity?? If dominance failed → `key = parsed.base` = \"bragantino\" → new entity! Why would dominance fail? base_share: total = sum of appearances over _by_base[\"bragantino\"] — hmm — WAIT: `_by_base` also includes the BASE of the suffixed NAME parse — for \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" (br file), parse_name → base \"redbullbragantino\" → registered under base \"redbullbragantino\" ✓ separate. Fine.\n\nHmm — but appearances: br \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" rows count toward redbullbragantinosp (114) ✓. cup \"Bragantino - SP\" → suffix alias → redbullbragantinosp ✓ +few. cup \"Bragantino - PA\" → bragantinopa +10. share = 114+X / (114+X+10) ≈ 0.92 ≥ 0.75 ✓ should resolve!!\n\nUNLESS — the Phase 1 registration of brf \"Bragantino\" happens when _by_base[\"bragantino\"] = {bragantinopa ONLY}?? Order: br → novo → cup → lib → brf. Cup registers \"Bragantino - SP\" (suffix alias → redbullbragantinosp, base bucket \"bragantino\" ✓) BEFORE brf ✓. Hmm — OR the bug: in register(), the suffix-alias path adds `self._by_base[parsed.base].add(key)` — parsed.base = \"bragantino\" ✓...\n\nWAIT. Let me recheck register()'s stateless branch:\n\n```python\nelse:\n    alias_target = ALIASES.get(parsed.base)\n    if alias_target and alias_target in self.teams:\n        key = alias_target\n    elif not parsed.base:\n        key = fold(raw)\n    else:\n        candidates = self._by_base.get(parsed.base, set())\n        if len(candidates) == 1:\n            key = next(iter(candidates))\n        elif len(candidates) > 1:\n            ranked = sorted(candidates, key=...appearances, reverse=True)\n            if self.base_share(ranked[0]) >= self.DOMINANCE:\n                key = ranked[0]\n            else:\n                key = parsed.base\n        else:\n            key = parsed.base\n```\n\nFor \"Bragantino\": ALIASES.get(\"bragantino\") — NOT in ALIASES (only full name \"Red Bull Bragantino\" is) → None. candidates = {\"redbullbragantinosp\", \"bragantinopa\"} → ranked → redbull first (114 vs 10) → share = 124/134 = 0.925 ≥ 0.75 → key = redbullbragantinosp ✓✓. So it SHOULD work! But the 2022 matches created NEW (source BR-Foot)...\n\nOH WAIT. I see it — **the standings showed a bare entity earlier? Let me actually check the keys of those brf-new matches.** The display \"Bragantino\" — display of redbullbragantinosp is... finalize_displays: base \"bragantino\"... wait — redbullbragantinosp's base is \"redbullbragantino\" (from its FIRST registration via br file \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\")! Its base is NOT \"bragantino\". So base_share(redbullbragantinosp) = appearances/(sum over _by_base[\"redbullbragantino\"]) = itself only → 1.0. But in the stateless resolution I rank candidates from _by_base[\"bragantino\"] = {redbullbragantinosp, bragantinopa} — ranked[0] = redbull (114+) → base_share uses redbull's OWN base bucket (\"redbullbragantino\") → share 1.0 ≥ 0.75 → resolves ✓✓✓. Hmm STILL should work!\n\nSo why didn't it?! Let me just test register directly. Maybe the issue is ordering: brf \"Bragantino\" registration in Phase 1 — WAIT, actually — the brf-new MATCHES were created in Phase 2 `_ref(row[\"home\"])` → register(...) — same logic ✓. Hmm, let me test:\n\nregistry.register(\"Bragantino\") after loading → what key? Actually — OH NO. I bet I see it: **`_by_base` adds under `parsed.base` only when creating a NEW team**:\n\n```python\nteam = self.teams.get(key)\nif team is None:\n    team = Team(key=key, base=parsed.base, ...)\n    self.teams[key] = team\n    if parsed.base:\n        self._by_base.setdefault(parsed.base, set()).add(key)\n```\n\nWhen cup registers \"Bragantino - SP\" → suffix alias → key redbullbragantinosp — but was redbullbragantinosp ALREADY created by br file (\"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" → base \"redbullbragantino\" → key \"redbullbragantinosp\")? YES (br loads first). So team EXISTS → skip creation → **`_by_base[\"bragantino\"]` never gets redbullbragantinosp added!!** The base-bucket only updates at creation. So _by_base[\"bragantino\"] = {bragantinopa} only → brf \"Bragantino\" → candidates len 1 → **bragantinopa**?!?! WAIT — then brf Bragantino resolves to bragantinopa (the PARÁ club)!! Let me verify... the 2022 brf-new \"Bragantino\" matches — their key = bragantinopa?? That would ALSO mean copa \"Bragantino - SP\" rows attached to redbullbragantinosp ✓ (suffix alias direct) but brf stateless → bragantinopa ✗✗ WRONG CLUB.\n\nFIX: in register(), always add key to _by_base[parsed.base] bucket (not only at creation):\n```python\nif parsed.base:\n    self._by_base.setdefault(parsed.base, set()).add(key)\n```\nunconditionally. That fixes the bucket. Then candidates = {redbullbragantinosp, bragantinopa} → ranked → redbull (more appearances) → base_share(redbullbragantinosp) — hmm — share computed over _by_base[team.base] where team.base = \"redbullbragantino\" → only itself → 1.0 ✓ resolves ✓.\n\nBUT WAIT — there's a subtlety: with the bucket fix, base_share for \"bragantinopa\" = appearances / sum(_by_base[\"bragantino\"]) — bragantinopa.team.base = \"bragantino\" ✓ its bucket now includes redbull → share tiny ✓ fine.\n\nBut hold on — the dominance check for redbullbragantinosp uses the WRONG bucket (its own base \"redbullbragantino\" — share 1.0). That's actually the behavior I want here (auto-resolve to the majority), but semantically muddy: ranked[0] by appearances among candidates, then share computed on ranked[0]'s OWN base bucket. For the Vasco-style cases (flamengo: base \"flamengo\" bucket {flamengorj, flamengopi} — redbull-style mismatch only when base names differ). Eh — it works. But cleaner: compute share over the CANDIDATES bucket: share = appearances[best] / sum(appearances[c] for c in candidates). Let me refactor base_share to accept an optional candidates set... Actually simpler: in the register/resolve dominance checks, compute share directly over the bucket being examined:\n\n```python\ndef _dominant(self, candidates: set[str]) -> str | None:\n    if not candidates: return None\n    total = sum(self.teams[k].appearances for k in candidates if k in self.teams)\n    if not total: return None\n    best = max(candidates, key=lambda k: self.teams[k].appearances)\n    if self.teams[best].appearances / total >= self.DOMINANCE:\n        return best\n    return None\n```\nUse in register (stateless ambiguous), resolve (steps 4 & 5), and finalize_displays (share over own base bucket — keep base_share for that? finalize uses base_share(team.key) over team's own bucket ✓ fine).\n\nLet me rewrite those parts of normalize.py:\n1. register: unconditional bucket add + use _dominant.\n2. resolve: use _dominant.\n3. Keep base_share for finalize (or make finalize use _dominant over the team's base bucket — equivalent).\n\nAlso — the earlier `vitoria` bare entity (393 matches): after suffix alias fix, _by_base[\"vitoria\"] = {vitoriaba} (vitoriaes never created since aliased) → stateless \"Vitoria\" → unique → vitoriaba ✓.\n\nThen rerun. Also 2015 +1 (one brf fixture not in br 2015 — probably a genuine data discrepancy (postponed match with changed date/pair? no — pair-keyed... a brf 2015 pair that br 2015 lacks — maybe a rescheduled match replayed? whatever, +1 noise, fine). 2009: 379 (novo internal dupe skipped ✓). 2023: 377 (brf incomplete by 3 — fine).\n\nAfter this, totals should be: 2012-2022 = 380 ✓ (except 2015: 381, 2020-2022: 380 after bragantino fix ✓).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b93f9c9001drPQWDmySt8nHV","sessionID":"ses_fa4885bb0ffe9T9m197C2RPPvh","messageID":"msg_05b936f5a001RSTksy02S0cRzY"}}
