ance ✓ — brf registers ~397×... hmm 794?? And \"Sport\" 684 = br (152+... br Sport-PE ~152×... whatever).\n\nWhich display do I actually want? \"Sport\" (bare, common). The counts are polluted by source duplication (brf rows double-registering existing fixtures). Screw it — simplest robust choice: prefer the variant from the PRIMARY source... Overthinking. Just prefer SHORTEST bare variant with accent priority: score = (accent, -length, -spaces)?? For \"Vitória\" (1) ✓; for Sport: (0,-5,0) vs (0,-11,-1) → -5 > -11 → \"Sport\" ✓. But then a club whose bare variants are \"Retrô\" vs... shorter is usually the common name in this dataset ✓. Check: vitoriaba bare variants: \"Vitória\"(accent) ✓ wins anyway. palmeirassp: bare \"Palmeiras\" only ✓. saopaulosp: \"Sao Paulo\" (br), \"São Paulo\" (novo, accent ✓) → accented wins ✓. gremiors: \"Grêmio\" accent ✓ vs \"Gremio\" → ✓. corinthianssp: \"Corinthians\" ✓. flamengorj: \"Flamengo\" ✓. botafogorj: \"Botafogo\" ✓. santossp: \"Santos\" ✓. fluminenserj: \"Fluminense\" ✓. vascodagamarj: \"Vasco da Gama\" (only bare variant; brf \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" suffixed-skip ✓) → \"Vasco da Gama\" ✓ nice (current display \"Vasco\" — from novo bare \"Vasco\" — shorter — hmm -12 vs -5: \"Vasco\" (0,-5) wins over \"Vasco da Gama\" (0,-12) → \"Vasco\" ✓ also fine).\n\nSo: bare score = (accent, -length). And refresh_display score (for non-finalized teams): (accent, has_state, -length)? Currently (accent, has_state, -length) with -length meaning shorter preferred?? Current refresh: `(-length)` in tuple position 3 — let me check... refresh score: `(1 if has_accent else 0, 1 if has_state else 0, -length)`?? Looking at the code I wrote: `return (1 if has_accent else 0, 1 if has_state else 0, -length)` — hmm I need to verify. Earlier refresh picked \"Vitoria ES\"... let me look at the current file section. Also make refresh consistent: (accent, has_state, -length).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b98cdb4001gsRhVik4E4vF1t","sessionID"