{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788243245877,"sessionID":"ses_fa4885bb0ffe9T9m197C2RPPvh","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-87cbf00b9f804cbc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py","newString":"        self._register_all_teams()\n        self._build_matches()\n        self._stamp_copa_finals()\n        self.registry.finalize_displays()\n        self._refresh_display_names()\n        self._load_players()\n        for match in self.matches:\n            self._team_matches[match.home.key].append(match)\n            self._team_matches[match.away.key].append(match)","oldString":"        self._register_all_teams()\n        self._build_matches()\n        self.registry.finalize_displays()\n        self._refresh_display_names()\n        self._load_players()\n        for match in self.matches:\n            self._team_matches[match.home.key].append(match)\n            self._team_matches[match.away.key].append(match)"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n@@ -97,8 +97,9 @@\n self._team_matches: dict[str, list[Match]] = defaultdict(list)\n \n self._register_all_teams()\n self._build_matches()\n+self._stamp_copa_finals()\n self.registry.finalize_displays()\n self._refresh_display_names()\n self._load_players()\n for match in self.matches:\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d2915a49bf69/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n@@ -97,8 +97,9 @@\n self._team_matches: dict[str, list[Match]] = defaultdict(list)\n \n self._register_all_teams()\n self._build_matches()\n+self._stamp_copa_finals()\n self.registry.finalize_displays()\n self._refresh_display_names()\n self._load_players()\n for match in self.matches:\n","additions":1,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/loader.py","time":{"start":1788243245875,"end":1788243245877}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Libertadores \"final\" now correctly matches only 15 finals ✓. But **Copa do Brasil stage=\"final\" returns nothing** — because copa round labels are \"Round 8\" (not \"Final\"). The finals tool handles copa specially. For search_matches, users asking \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" would use stage=\"final\" → empty. Improvement: in the copa loader, ADD a stage label for final-round matches? The final round per season is computed... at load time I can compute: for copa matches from the cup file, per season, the max played round → set stage=\"Final\" on those matches; max-1 → \"Semifinals\"? Hmm — 2012 cup had 6 rounds (final=6); 2016: 7 rounds. The max round ≠ consistent meaning across seasons (\"Round 6\" in 2012 = final; \"Round 6\" in 2013 = quarterfinal?). Setting stage on max-round matches per season = \"Final\" is defensible (it IS the deciding round of that season in the data).\n\nBut careful: 2021 cup — max PLAYED round from cup file = 3 (round 4 all NA) → round-3 matches labeled \"Final\"?? WRONG (round 3 = round of 16!). Hmm — but wait: rounds 1-3 played, round 4 NA → but after brf fill, round-4 NA rows may be FILLED with brf scores... then max played round = 4 → \"Final\" — still wrong (round 4 2021 = round of 16!). Ugh — 2021+ cup format: 2021 rounds: 1, 2, 3, 4=round-of-16?? Let me think: 2021 Copa: 92 clubs; rounds: 1st (46), 2nd (23+bye), 3rd (32?), ... the cup file 2021: rounds 1-4, 108 matches: r1: 40? r2: ..., r3: ..., r4: 16 matches (8 ties × 2 legs). Actually from earlier NA analysis: round 4 = 16 NA matches = 8 two-leg ties = round of 16 (last-16). So 2021 file lacks QF onward (they're in brf without rounds). So \"max round\" heuristic for 2021 = wrong.\n\nBetter stage labels for copa: use round-number → name mapping per era... The actual Copa formats: 2012-2015: rounds 1..8 where 8=final? 2012 had 6 rounds?? Data: 2012 max round 6 — but 2012 copa DID have more rounds... 2012: 64 teams: r1(32), r2(16), r3(8=R16), r4(QF), r5(SF), r6(FINAL two legs) ✓ 6 rounds. 2013-2015: 87 teams: 8 rounds (r8=final) ✓. 2016-2020: r7=final? 2016 max 7 ✓ (SF=6? no wait 2016: 86 teams... r7=final ✓ per data). 2017-2020: r8=final ✓. 2021: rounds 1-4 only (r4=R16!). 2022-2023: cup file has NO data (brf only, no rounds).\n\nSo per-season final-round = max PLAYED round, EXCEPT 2021 (max played = 4 but that's R16, real final is in brf-only). Hmm — my `finals` tool already handles this via `_infer_copa_final` fallback ✓. For search_matches stage matching on copa, I'll set stage labels at LOAD time using the same logic as the finals tool: per season, compute final-round matches (cup-file rounds: max played round if that round is \"late\" (≥6?)... hmm 2012's final = round 6 ✓ ≥6; 2016 final = 7 ✓. For 2021: max played round after fills = 4 → 4 < 6 → DON'T label as final → rely on brf-inferred? The brf-inferred final (2 matches) — I could label those stage=\"Final\" too!\n\nSimplest robust approach: after loading everything, run the finals-identification logic (same as service) ONCE and stamp `stage` on copa matches identified as finals: (a) cup-file max-round matches when max round ≥ 5, (b) brf-inferred tail finals for seasons without (a). Then search stage=\"final\" works for copa, and the finals tool can ALSO use the stamped stage (simpler + consistent).\n\nHmm — wait, \"max round ≥ 5\": 2012: 6 ✓ final. What about 2013-2015 (max 8) ✓; 2016-2020 (7 or 8) ✓. 2021: 4 → skip → brf tail inference: last 2 matches of 2021 = Atlético-MG vs Athletico ✓ (from brf, no round labels — stamp stage=\"Final\"). 2022: brf tail: Corinthians/Flamengo ✓. 2023: São Paulo/Flamengo ✓.\n\nBut careful — the brf-inferred final for seasons 2014-2020 where cup-file finals exist: cup-file stamping wins (max round ≥5) ✓ and tail inference skipped. What about seasons where cup-file max round ≥5 but is NOT the final?? e.g. 2012 r6 = final ✓... could r5 2012 (SF) be max if final unplayed? Not the case in data ✓.\n\nEdge: 2020 cup — final = round 8, played (Palmeiras-Grêmio Mar 2021) ✓ max played round 8 ✓.\n\nAlso stamp \"Semifinals\" for final_round-1? Nice-to-have; skip — stage search for \"semifinal\" on copa would fail but that's OK (rounds still searchable). Hmm — actually let me also stamp semifinals = round (final-1) when final round ≥ 6... The 2012 SF = round 5 ✓. 2021: no. Keep it simple: only stamp \"Final\".\n\nImplementation: in loader after _build_extended_stats (all copa matches loaded + filled): \n\n```python\ndef _stamp_copa_finals(self):\n    comp_matches = [m for m in self.matches if m.competition == 'copa_do_brasil']\n    for season in sorted({m.season for m in comp_matches if m.season}):\n        season_matches = [m for m in comp_matches if m.season == season]\n        rounds = [int(m.round_label.split()[-1]) for m in season_matches\n                  if m.round_label and m.round_label.startswith('Round') and m.played and m.round_label.split()[-1].isdigit()]\n        if rounds and max(rounds) >= 5:\n            fr = max(rounds)\n            for m in season_matches:\n                if m.round_label == f'Round {fr}':\n                    m.stage = 'Final'\n            continue\n        # infer from latest two played matches (two-legged final)\n        played = sorted([m for m in season_matches if m.played and m.date], key=lambda m: m.date)\n        if len(played) >= 2:\n            tail = played[-2:]\n            teams = {tail[0].home.key, tail[0].away.key, tail[1].home.key, tail[1].away.key}\n            if len(teams) == 2:\n                for m in tail: m.stage = 'Final'\n```\n\nWait — for seasons where cup-file final exists (2012-2020), tail inference skipped ✓. For 2021: rounds max played... after brf fills round 4 might be played → max=4 <5 → tail inference → Atlético-MG/Athletico 2021 ✓. BUT the tail inference on 2021: last two played = 2021-12-12 AtlMG 4-0 Athletico & 2021-12-16 Athletico 1-2 AtlMG ✓ 2 teams ✓ stamp ✓. 2022: 10-13 COR 0-0 FLA + 10-20 FLA 1-1 COR ✓. 2023: 09-17 FLA 0-1 SAO + 09-24 SAO 1-1 FLA ✓.\n\nHmm — but what about seasons where cup-file finals exist AND brf has later matches?? e.g. 2020: cup final round 8 (Mar 2021) — brf copa 2020 rows: QF/SF/final — all joined/filled into cup rows ✓ same matches ✓. The stamping by round works ✓ (round 8 2020 = the final ✓). But wait — is the max round in the DATA for 2020 really 8? Earlier per-season cup counts showed 2020: 190 matches — rounds? The final was played (Palmeiras 2-0 Grêmio aggregate from earlier print ✓ round 8 ✓).\n\nHmm — one more: 2017 cup final round 8 = Flamengo vs Cruzeiro (agg 1-1, penalties) ✓ data has it ✓.\n\nThen the service `_copa_finals` can use stage=='Final' directly (stamped), simplifying. And `_copa_final_round_matches` stays for... I'll refactor `_copa_finals` to use stamped stage. Actually — keep `_copa_final_round_matches` + `_infer_copa_final` as the stamping logic IN THE LOADER, and `_copa_finals` reads stage=='Final'. Let me also double check the \"max round >= 5\" guard against 2012 (6 rounds, final=6 ✓ stamp). What if some season's cup data is truncated mid-competition with max played round ≥ 5 that ISN'T the final? E.g. hypothetical: 2019 data ends at SF (round 7) → stamp round 7 as Final — wrong-ish but data-limited. From the actual numbers: 2019: 136 matches, rounds 1-8 with final played ✓ (data complete). OK.\n\nAlso — I realize there's an ordering subtlety: stamping happens after all matches loaded (including brf fills) — in SoccerData.__init__ after _build_matches() ✓ before _refresh_display_names (order irrelevant).\n\nLet me implement.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b9a72e3001aBogD8tBy47KOT","sessionID":"ses_fa4885bb0ffe9T9m197C2RPPvh","messageID":"msg_05b99e039001XNNTvI8o88HOyh"}}
