ngo\", \"Fluminense\", limit=0).splitlines()[-1])\nprint('===')\nprint(svc.search_players(nationality=\"Brazil\", min_overall=88)[:300])\nprint('===')\nn = sum(1 for m in data.matches if m.stats)\nprint('matches with extended stats:', n)\nprint('teams:', len(data.registry.teams), 'players:', len(data.players))\nbraz = sum(1 for p in data.players if p.nationality=='Brazil')\nprint('brazilian players:', braz)\n# 2009 single dupe check\nprint('serie A 2009 count:', sum(1 for m in data.matches if m.competition=='brasileirao' and m.season==2009))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788243378139,"end":1788243378157}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The RAW BR-Foot data itself says Palmeiras 2023 = 19W 10D 8L (67 pts, 37 matches). And 2022 = 23W 12D 3L = 81 pts ✓ (real: Palmeiras 2022 champion with 81 ✓✓!). So the pipeline is CORRECT for 2022; the 2023 raw data in this Kaggle dataset is simply an incomplete/variant snapshot (37 matches for Palmeiras, no dups). My memory said Palmeiras 2023 = 82 pts — and indeed the real 2023 champion was Palmeiras with 82... The dataset's 2023 numbers (67 pts Grêmio 68) don't match the real final table. But wait — if Palmeiras has 37 matches (1 missing) and 19W... real 24W. Even with the missing match it'd be 20W max. So this dataset's 2023 Serie A results genuinely differ from the official record — OR the dataset captured the season mid-way with some results wrong... Hmm — interesting: 377 matches, all teams 37-38 games — season complete in shape but results differ. Could the brf 2023 Serie A be... COPIED from a different season?? Grêmio 68 pts \"champion\" — Grêmio hasn't been champion since 1996... Hmm — OR my month≤2 rule reassignments? 2023 has no Jan/Feb. OR season mislabeled: what if brf \"2023\" rows are actually 2024 season data?? 2024 Brasileirão: champion Botafogo! Data shows Botafogo 5th... no.\n\nHmm — wait. What if the 377 rows include SOME duplicated pairs across DIFFERENT spellings that got merged (pair keys same after normalization) — no dups in RAW pairs (home/away raw strings) ✓ 377 unique. But my LOADED 2023 = 377 too ✓ consistent.\n\nWhat about real 2023 final table: 1. Palmeiras 82, 2. Grêmio 72, 3. Atlético-MG 70?, 4. Flamengo 70?, 5. Botafogo 64... vs data: Grêmio 68, Palmeiras 67, Atlético 66, Flamengo 66, Botafogo 64. Botafogo 64 ✓ EXACT vs real. Flamengo real 2023: 66 pts?? Hmm — maybe! Let me reconsider: real 2023: Botafogo 5th 64 ✓; Flamengo 4th 66?? hmm I thought 70... Atlético-MG 3rd 66?? I thought 70. Palmeiras 82 vs data 67 — 15 pts gap?! Grêmio 72 vs 68. If Palmeiras real 82: 24W 10D 4L. Data 19W 10D 8L: differences: −5W +4L = 9 flipped/missing... with only 1 missing match. So either the dataset is flat-out wrong about 5 Palmeiras 2023 results, or my memory is wrong.\n\nActually — you know what, let me just check ONE Palmeiras 2023 result I'm sure about: the 2023 title clincher: Palmeiras 1-1 Cruzeiro?? no... Final round 2023: Palmeiras played Bahia away? Hmm. Or the Derby: Corinthians vs Palmeiras 2023? Or: Palmeiras vs Botafogo 2023 (Botafogo's collapse game: Botafogo lost 0-3?? no — Flamengo 3-0 Botafogo...). I can't reliably verify from memory; the DATASET is the ground truth for this benchmark. The spec says \"Standings by season (calculated from match results)\" — my implementation computes exactly that ✓. If the Kaggle 2023 snapshot differs from official history, that's a data-quality note for the README, not a code bug. Hmm — wait, actually, one more hypothesis: maybe brf 2023 rows are from BEFORE the season ended + include some POSTPONED matches' PREDICTED/scheduled results?... 377 matches can't be pre-Season-end (would be ~300).\n\nHmm, actually — hold on, let me reconsider: is it possible the 2023 brf rows are fine but include DUPLICATE entries of the same matches under DIFFERENT home/away (double-counted reversed)?? No dups in raw pairs; 377 rows ≈ 380 − 3.\n\nLet me sanity-check one known 2023 result: 2023-11-01?? Copa... in Serie A: \"Palmeiras 2-1 Flamengo\"? hmm. What about the LAST round 2023-12-07: data shows Cruzeiro 1-1 Palmeiras?? wait earlier print: \"2023-12-07: Cruzeiro 1-1 Palmeiras\" hmm no — it showed \"2023-12-07: Cruzeiro 1-1 Palmeiras (Brasileirão Serie A)\"?? Actually the first smoke test printed \"2023-12-07: Cruzeiro 1-1 Palmeiras\"?? Let me look: earlier search_matches Palmeiras date range print: \"2023-12-07: Cruzeiro 1-1 Palmeiras\". Real final round 2023: Palmeiras... hmm I don't remember.\n\nDecision: trust the dataset (it's the provided ground truth); tests assert data-derived expectations (e.g., 2019 table matches the SPEC example ✓ — that's the important anchor; 2022 relegation/others from data). For 2023 I'll assert structural properties (20 teams, 377 matches, structure sane), not specific champions. Also worth checking 2020 relegated from data (printed earlier but got cut: \"egated\" tail). Let me get the 2020 bottom-4 + a couple more expectations, then WRITE THE TESTS. No more rabbit-holing on 2023 data accuracy — one README note suffices: \"2023 Serie A figures reflect the provided snapshot\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b9c6d73001AcAcyCPPAHXeZs","sessionID":"ses_fa4885bb0ffe9T9m197C2RPPvh","messageID":"msg_05b9c1442001EqbZp4y293uOau"}}
