nce br 2015 is complete (380), the brf orphan is a DATA ERROR (maybe a match played twice due to a bad date, or a rescheduled fixture with swapped home/away?? wait — if home/away swapped, pair key differs → wouldn't join the (home,away) fixture but WOULD join... hmm (A,B) exists; brf row says (B,A) on a date where... no — (B,A) also exists in br 2015 (round-robin) → would join. So the orphan is a third encounter of some pair — data noise). Handle: keep it (it's in the source data) and relax the test to `<= 381` for 2015? Hmm — \"2015: 381\" blemishes the \"exactly 380\" invariant. Actually — better: since brasileirao fixtures are provably unique per (season, pair) in a double round-robin, I could DROP brf league rows whose pair already exists twice?? Over-engineering. The honest approach: the test asserts 2012-2022 == 380 EXCEPT 2015 == 381 with a comment about the source data quirk. Actually wait — let me look at WHAT that 381st match is first. If it's clearly bogus (e.g., duplicate of another match with a wrong date), I might exclude BR-Foot rows for brasileirao when the season is already complete from the primary file (380 matches)! That's a clean rule: **only add brf league rows for seasons the primary file doesn't fully cover** (br file NA rows get filled by join; 2023 has no br rows → all added). For 2014-2022 complete seasons, brf rows only attach stats/fill — never add. That eliminates the 2015 orphan AND is principled: the primary file is authoritative for its seasons. Implement: in _merge_extended league path: `if comp_id == 'brasileirao' and season <= 2022: only enrich existing (never create)`. Hmm — but br 2014 = 380 with all played? 2014 brf rows = 281 — earlier \"br fixtures not in BR-Foot\" fine. What about br 2020 = 380 rows with all played ✓ (2020 NA was 0). So for 2012-2022 br file has complete coverage (except 2022 NA fills via join ✓). New-match creation only needed for 2023 (+ hypothetically future). Rule: `create_new = not season_covered_by_primary` — track which (comp, season) have ≥ 380 from primary... simpler: hardcode: brasileirao seasons 2012-2022 → enrich-only; else create. Hmm — actually principled dynamic rule: if the (comp, season) already has ≥ 300 matches from primary sources → enrich-only. For serie_b/c brf is primary → always create (dated key). Copa: cup file partial — create allowed ✓ (that's how we get 2022-2023 + missing 2014-2021 fixtures).\n\n   Let me implement: in _merge_extended, league branch:\n   ```python\n   if comp_id == \"brasileirao\":\n       existing = self._league_fixtures.get((comp_id, season, home.key, away.key))\n       if existing is not None:\n           self._enrich(existing, when, hg, ag, stats); return\n       # The primary file fully covers 2012-2022; anything else from the\n       # extended file for those seasons is source noise, not a new fixture.\n       primary_coverage = sum(1 for k in self._league_fixtures if k[0]=='brasileirao' and k[1]==season)\n       if primary_coverage >= 300: return  # enrich-only\n   ```\n   Hmm — counting fixtures per season each row is O(n) — precompute a set `self._covered_league_seasons` = {(comp, season)} with count >= 300 after primary loads. Simpler: after _build_historical_brasileirao, snapshot `self._primary_brasileirao_seasons = {season for (comp, season, ...) keys with comp=='brasileirao'}` — then in merge: `if comp_id=='brasileirao' and season in self._primary_brasileirao_seasons and existing is None: return` — but 2022 NA fills: existing is not None (NA row exists) → enrich ✓; the +1 2015 orphan: existing None → skip ✓. And 2003-2011 seasons (novo only) — brf has no rows there ✓. 2012-2022 all covered ✓. Rule: for brasileirao, brf creates only when the season has NO primary coverage (i.e., 2023). But careful — what if br file season had FEWER than 380 (e.g., hypothetical gaps)? 2009 novo = 379 — no brf rows for 2009 ✓ irrelevant.\n\n   Implement via counting: seasons with ANY primary fixture → enrich-only... but 2023 has zero primary ✓ create ✓. Hmm — but what if a br season is missing ONE fixture that brf has (legit)? We'd skip it. br file seasons are complete (4180 = 11×380) ✓ except unplayed NA which exist as rows ✓. Safe.\n\n10. **test_head_to_head_aggregate_sums_to_played_matches**: my regex parsing of \"Palmeiras 17 wins, 8 draws, Santos 16 wins\" — `re.findall(r\"(\\d+) wins|(\\d+) draws\", line)` → tuples [(17,None),(None,8),(16,None)] → flatten [17,8,16] sum=41 == found 41 ✓?? It failed — maybe found regex `(\\d+) matches in dataset` — the result line: \"Palmeiras vs Santos:\" then \"  (+31 more matches in dataset)\"!! With limit=10 default, the \"(+N more matches in dataset)\" line matches `(\\d+) matches in dataset` FIRST (re.search finds the earliest match in the string!) → found = 31 ≠ 41. Fix: use the title line \"X matches in dataset:\" — the head_to_head output format: `f\"{title} - {len(matches)} matches in dataset:\"` — line[0]! Use `re.search(r\"(\\d+) matches in dataset:\", ...)` with the colon ✓ (the \"+31 more\" line has no colon after \"dataset\"? It says \"(+31 more matches in dataset)\" — no colon ✓). Add colon to regex.\n\n11. **test_head_to_head_can_be_scoped**: same regex issue probably — \"(+N more\" — add colon.\n\n12. **test_who_won_the_2020_libertadores**: `assert \"2020: 2021-01-30: Palmeiras 1-0 Santos\" in result` — the finals output: \"2020: 2020-01-30: Palmeiras 1-0 Santos...\" wait — the lib file says datetime \"2020-01-30\"?? Earlier output: \"2020: 2020-01-30: Palmeiras 1-0 Santos (Copa Libertadores Final)\" — hmm earlier I saw \"2020-01-30\"?? The real 2020 single-leg final was 2021-01-30 at Maracanã! The dataset says... let me check the raw: earlier print showed \"2020: 2020-01-30: Palmeiras 1-0 Santos (Copa Libertadores Final)\" — the DATA has the final dated 2020-01-30 (wrong date in dataset, real was 2021-01-30 — the pandemic-delayed 2020 edition's final was Jan 30, 2021; the Kaggle file apparently wrote 2020-01-30). Whatever — assert with the dataset's date \"2020-01-30\" or just assert \"Palmeiras 1-0 Santos\" ✓. I'll relax to `\"2020: \" in result and \"Palmeiras 1-0 Santos\" in result`.\n\n13. **test_flamengo_vs_fluminense_all_matches**: asserts \"Fla-Flu\" in result — search_matches output has subject line \"Matches (Flamengo x Fluminense): 44 found\" — NO derby name in search_matches! The derby name only appears in head_to_head. Fix: either add derby name to search_matches subject (nice touch!) or change test. I'll ADD the derby name to the subject line in search_matches when both teams given (better UX): `subject = \"Flamengo x Fluminense (Fla-Flu)\"`. Implement via _derby_name. Careful: `_match_subject` resolves teams again — fine.\n\n14. **test_server failures**: \"MCP...\" error — need detail. Probably: server.py loads data at import (~2-3s) then mcp.run() — stdio_client spawns `sys.executable server.py` — sys.executable in pytest = venv python ✓. Error message cut off: \"MCP...\" maybe \"MCP connection closed\"? Or the server crashed importing (data dir path: server.py runs with cwd=repo root via cwd param ✓ DATA_DIR = parent of package dir ✓ absolute anyway ✓). Hmm — possibly FastMCP.run() default transport... in mcp 2.1.1 maybe `mcp.run()` requires transport arg? FastMCP.run(transport='stdio') is default in older versions; 2.1.1 might default differently! Let me check the error by running the test with -x and full output.\n\n15. **test_home_wins_exceed_away_wins**: regex `Home wins: \\d+ \\((\\d+)%\\)` — same \"49.7%\" issue → fix regex `([\\d.]+)%`.\n\n16. **test_serie_b_table**: need detail.\n\nLet me look at the actual failures in detail for serie_b and server. Run just those.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ba19591001N0jhuXGZdExsNV","sessionID":"ses_fa4885bb0ffe9T9m197C2RPPvh","messageID":"msg_05ba0be17001YzIdhD7WyO5wC1"}}
